===============================================================================
ACCEPTABLE USE ADDENDUM
===============================================================================

A note on what this addendum is and is not, stated up front so nothing
below is misread as more than it claims to be: this addendum records
the authors' intent and values. It does not narrow, qualify, or add
conditions to the MIT grant above. Every right granted above — to use,
copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and sell copies of
this Software, for any lawful purpose, including commercial purposes —
remains fully and unconditionally available. Nothing below is a license
term whose violation could void the grant above; it is the authors
explaining, plainly, why this project exists and what they hope it is
never used for. Whether that changes anyone's mind is not something a
text file can control, and the authors hold no illusion that it will.
It is written anyway, because staying silent would misrepresent the
authors' actual views, and because a clear statement of intent has
value on its own, independent of whether it deters anyone.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. What this project is for
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Rusk exists to make it easier for a Rust developer to build real,
useful, legitimate Android applications — games, utilities, tools,
libraries — without hand-rolling a JNI bridge, an NDK toolchain
integration, an APK packaging pipeline, and a signing workflow from
scratch every time. Every design choice in this codebase, from the
build-cache architecture to the render loop to the Java interop layer,
serves that one goal: lowering the cost of building software that a
person installs on their own device, for their own benefit, with their
own informed consent to what that software does.

That framing also gives the clearest view of what this project is not
for. A tool that makes legitimate app development faster and safer, by
construction, also makes illegitimate app development faster — a JNI
bridge that safely calls a weather API can just as safely call a
command-and-control server; a code-signing helper that lets a solo
developer ship a game to friends can just as easily sign a trojan. This
is not a flaw unique to Rusk; it is a property of every general-purpose
development tool that has ever existed, from a compiler to a text
editor to a packaging format. The authors cannot engineer that dual-use
property out of a build toolchain any more than a hardware store can
engineer it out of a hammer. What the authors can do is say plainly
which use they built this for, and which use they did not.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. What the authors are asking Software built with Rusk not to be
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The authors ask — as a stated preference, not an enforceable license
condition — that the Software, and any code generated by it (via
`rusk new`, code generators such as `rusk-androidgen`/`rusk-jnigen`, or
any other tool in this workspace), not be used to build, package, sign,
or distribute:

  (a) Malware in general — viruses and self-replicating code; trojans
      that misrepresent their function; worms; rootkits; and any
      program whose real behavior is deliberately hidden from, or
      misrepresented to, the person running it.

  (b) Spyware and stalkerware — software that monitors a device's
      location, messages, calls, camera, microphone, or browsing
      activity and reports that data to a third party without the
      device owner's informed, freely given consent. This specifically
      includes tools marketed for covertly tracking a partner, family
      member, or employee, regardless of how their marketing frames
      that purpose.

  (c) Stealers and credential harvesters — software built to extract
      passwords, session tokens, wallet keys, or payment data from a
      device without the account holder's knowledge, and send that data
      to an attacker-controlled destination.

  (d) Ransomware and extortionware — software that encrypts or
      withholds access to a device or its data to demand payment, or
      threatens to leak exfiltrated data unless paid.

  (e) Unauthorized security-bypass tooling — code built to defeat
      authentication or exploit a system the operator does not own and
      has no explicit authorization to test. This is not an objection
      to security research conducted with proper authorization (a bug
      bounty, an engagement letter, a CTF, one's own lab); the line is
      authorization, not the technical nature of the code.

  (f) Botnet or command-and-control infrastructure — components that
      take remote instructions from an operator and act on a device
      without its owner's knowledge, including using that device's
      network or compute for the operator's benefit rather than the
      owner's.

  (g) Deceptive or fraudulent apps more broadly — fake "cracked"
      software bundled with hidden payloads, fake utility apps whose
      real function is unrelated to (or harmful to) what they claim to
      do, or apps that misrepresent their publisher to exploit trust in
      a different, legitimate brand.

  (h) Anything built specifically to target, track, or endanger a named
      individual.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Why the authors are asking this
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3.1 The asymmetry of harm. Software built with a tool like this one runs
on someone's phone — the single object most people own that holds the
densest record of their life: messages, photos, banking, location,
account access. Malicious software there is not an abstract policy
violation; it is a concrete violation of one specific person's privacy,
finances, or safety, and the cost of it falls entirely on someone who
had no say in the decision to build the tool that harmed them. The
person who writes stalkerware does not experience being watched. That
asymmetry — the builder insulated, the target bearing the full cost —
is, in the authors' view, the central problem with malicious software,
independent of any question of legality.

3.2 Trust is the actual product of an SDK. A toolkit like this one is a
trust amplifier: one careful decision made once in a shared library —
correct exception handling, verified downloads, platform-correct
certificate checks — protects everyone building on top of it. Malicious
use inverts that same leverage, turning shared convenience into shared
scale for harm. That inversion is exactly what this section asks not to
happen.

3.3 The people hit hardest are rarely the imagined targets. In practice,
consumer spyware and credential-stealing malware overwhelmingly land on
people with the least power in their situation — a partner in an
abusive relationship, an employee tracked without disclosure, a relative
targeted by a fake support app. These are not rhetorical hypotheticals;
they are the ordinary, common reality of what this category of software
does, far more often than the more dramatic "state-sponsored" framing
that usually dominates security headlines.

3.4 Silence has a cost too. Saying nothing at all would be a normal,
common choice — most permissively licensed projects do. The authors
chose to write this down anyway, so that no one reading this project —
a contributor, a user, anyone evaluating it — has to guess where its
authors stand.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Why this section cannot, and does not claim to, stop anyone
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

It would be dishonest to write Section 3 and not also say plainly: none
of it is a technical or legal barrier. Someone determined to build
malicious software with this SDK can do so. This is source code that
runs entirely on someone else's machine, with no telemetry and no
mechanism for the authors to detect or block any particular use.
Nothing here can be "enforced" the way a hosted service might revoke
access. A person willing to build malware is, by definition, already
willing to disregard rules that carry real legal weight — the statutes
in the next section, which carry criminal penalties, not a merely
violated software preference. If that doesn't deter someone, a
permissively licensed project's stated wishes certainly won't. This
section isn't asked to do enforcement work it structurally cannot do —
only to make the authors' position unambiguous.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. The legal backdrop this addendum sits alongside
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Separately from this license, building or distributing malicious
software of the kinds described in Section 2 is, in most jurisdictions,
a criminal matter under laws that exist entirely independent of any
software license and remain fully applicable regardless of what this
file says. This is general context, not legal advice — consult a
qualified lawyer for anything beyond general awareness.

  - In the United States, the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act criminalizes
    unauthorized computer access and the transmission of malicious
    code; many states have parallel computer-crime statutes of their
    own.

  - In the United Kingdom, the Computer Misuse Act 1990 criminalizes
    unauthorized access, unauthorized acts impairing a computer's
    operation, and — notably — making or supplying tools intended for
    such offenses.

  - Across the European Union, member states are required to
    criminalize illegal system and data interference, and the
    production or distribution of tools built for that purpose, each
    implemented through national criminal codes.

  - Data-protection regimes such as the EU's GDPR independently make it
    unlawful to covertly collect personal data without a lawful basis
    and the subject's consent — the core function of spyware — as a
    data-protection matter separate from any computer-crime statute.

  - Distributing an app through a platform like Google Play while
    misrepresenting its function, or in violation of that platform's
    policies on malware or deceptive behavior, is additionally a
    contract and platform-policy matter, separate from criminal law,
    that can lead to account termination and referral to law
    enforcement where the conduct is itself criminal.

None of this depends on this project existing. It's restated here only
so that a reading of "it's just a permissive SDK license, so there's no
consequence" gets corrected in the same document that granted the
permissive license.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. A note to a developer who is on the fence
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

If you're reading this because you're genuinely weighing building
something from Section 2, the authors would rather try to talk you out
of it than just say no and hope that's enough.

The device you'd target belongs to a real, specific person whose life
would be made worse by what you're building — not an abstraction. If
the plan involves someone you know personally, that is close to the
most common real profile for this category of software, and it's worth
asking honestly what covertly monitoring someone says about whatever
problem you think it's solving.

The skill it takes to build a working stealer or a convincing fake app
is real, transferable, and in high legal demand: security research,
authorized penetration testing, detection engineering, incident
response all use the same underlying skill in the other direction. The
distance between "I can build something that steals credentials" and
"I can build something that detects and stops that, legally and well
paid" is usually smaller than it looks, and the second path has no
victim and no criminal exposure.

If what's driving this is anger, desperation, or a sense the target
deserves it — those feelings are worth taking seriously on their own
terms, addressed directly, rather than routed through an action that
won't actually resolve them and is discovered far more often than
people expect.

None of that is guaranteed to land. It costs nothing to have said it
plainly, in the same file granting the software permission to be used
at all.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. Interaction with the MIT grant above
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

To remove any remaining ambiguity: Sections 1 through 8 of this
addendum are non-binding as a matter of the license itself. They create
no condition, covenant, or restriction on the rights granted in the MIT
text above. The MIT terms are the complete, operative grant; this
addendum is context, not a modification of that grant's scope. The
authors considered, and rejected, a more restrictive "ethical source"
style license that would attempt to legally prohibit certain uses,
because such licenses are generally not considered open source under
standard definitions, are legally untested as real enforcement
mechanisms in most of the scenarios above, and — in the authors'
judgment — are no more effective at actually preventing misuse than a
clearly stated, honest, non-binding position, while being meaningfully
worse for the overwhelming majority of people using this SDK for its
actual purpose.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. Closing
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

If you build something with Rusk, the authors ask — but, consistent
with everything above, do not require — that you leave attribution to
the Rusk SDK in your own project's documentation, and that you consider
contributing fixes back for bugs you find.

And if what you build is a game, a tool, a small utility that makes
some part of someone's day better — that, unambiguously, is what this
project was for. Thank you for building it.
